description:
src/lib.rs:24-39 defines:
```
pub trait JmapCalendarsExt {
    fn with_calendars_session(&self, session: Session) -> SessionClient;
}
impl JmapCalendarsExt for JmapClient { ... }
```

This is the canonical extension-trait idiom (orphan-rule workaround for adding a method to a foreign type). PLAN.md (lines 65-84) intended the trait to host all 19 methods. The shipped design moved the methods to inherent impls on `SessionClient` (defined in this crate), leaving the trait with one method.

Two consequences:
1. The trait achieves nothing the orphan rule requires now — `SessionClient` is local, the inherent impl block on `JmapClient` is forced through the trait but the trait could just BE the with_calendars_session function that takes (&JmapClient, Session): `pub fn with_calendars_session(client: &JmapClient, session: Session) -> SessionClient`. Free function, no trait import needed at call site.

2. Callers must remember `use jmap_calendars_client::JmapCalendarsExt;` to access ONE method. Trait imports are a known papercut for new users.

If you want the chained `client.with_calendars_session(...)` syntax, the trait is the only way (orphan rule). That's a real ergonomic win and probably worth the import cost. But document the design decision so a future contributor doesn't 'simplify' it (or DO simplify it to a free function — the .with_calendars_session(...) chain is not load-bearing).

Filing as P3 idiomatic-rust/opinion-adjacent. Either choice is defensible.
